[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. The reports merged into it describe
more than one problem, and each needs a fix of its own. Nobody mis-read anything: the merge rule
never reads a body, so a ticket can only ever be as sharp as its subject lines, and these subject
lines are shared across all of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser closes most of this ticket and leaves two problems open, one of which the fix
can make worse.
</Callout>

## What the reports describe

<SplitTable ticket={4471} />

Every number above is counted by the component from the desk's own export, with the desk's own
rules. Nothing in the words below restates one.

## Why the one-day group is not a swap

A swapped date and a date one day out look alike in a subject line and nothing alike in a body.
A swap turns 7 April into 4 July: the two numbers trade places. These reports have no second
number to trade:

- `R-41202` — the invite says Tuesday 12 May, the calendar says Monday 11 May. 12 and 11 are not
  each other's transpose.
- `R-41620` — Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday.
- `R-41344` — the client in Lisbon got Tuesday, the client in Toronto got Monday, for one meeting.
- `R-41209` — every booking in June is one day off, and only June.
- `R-41236` — the invite arrived before the booking was made.

Two timezones disagreeing about which day it is, and one month behaving differently from the
others, point at an offset, not at a parser. The desk already saw it: those ids are the
`oneDayMove` list in `desk-notes.json`. The kb files them under the cause their tenant implies,
because `causeOf` reads the tenant and the channel and never the words.

## Why the hint group survives the parser fix

`booking-v2` prints `DD/MM/YYYY` under its date field, and `booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. The
tenants on `booking-v3` all write the day first. Their people type the month first, because the
hint tells them to, and the form stores it the tenant's way. Every report in this group came
through the form and through no other channel.

So these dates are wrong in the opposite direction from the rest of the ticket. Teach the parser
to read each tenant's order and this group gets read the tenant's way too, which is not the way it
was typed. The hint and the reader have to agree first; either the hint matches how the form
reads, or the form reads what the hint promised.

## What the ticket's own table already shows

The incident page claims one cause in its words. Its own table, built from the data at render
time, prints two, and flags the second as *not this ticket — no ticket of its own*. It has been
doing that since before this page existed. The table was right and the words were not:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The tickets

A ticket covers a cause. `causes.ts` says which reports are in the wrong one: *a ticket is the
cause most of its reports share; the rest were merged into the wrong place*. `ticketCause` reads
#4471's cause off its reports and gets `parser`, so the `hint` reports are the ones in the wrong
place, and the table above says so in its own words.

- `parser` — [ticket #1](https://github.com/BlackShaira/kurs/issues/1), and on the kb it is
  [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) itself
- `hint` — [ticket #2](https://github.com/BlackShaira/kurs/issues/2), the ticket this cause
  never had
- `one-day` — no ticket. These reports are not in the wrong ticket, so `readAs` keeps them
  counted where they are filed and footnoted there, which is what the table above shows. The
  problem is named here instead, and the desk's own list of it is in `desk-notes.json`.

## What to do

1. Fix them in three changes, not one. #4471 stays the parser ticket, and its fix closes the
   `parser` row apart from the one-day reports counted inside it.
2. Ship the `hint` fix in the same release as the parser fix. On its own, each of the two moves
   that group from wrong to differently wrong.
3. Leave the counts where the desk files them, and leave the reports where they are. The ticket
   a report belongs to is `mergedInto`, and moving a report out of #4471 would take it out of the
   desk's view of #4471 altogether.
4. Decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line alone and
   accepts that one bug can land in several tickets. The reverse happened here. A decision is
   never edited once accepted, so this needs a new record, not a correction of that one.

## What this page does not settle

- Which offset causes the one-day group. The reports quoted above name two timezones and one
  month; that is enough to separate the problem, not enough to name its cause.
- Whether more reports belong to the one-day group than the desk listed. The list is the desk's
  own. Pattern matching over the bodies finds four of them and misses `R-41236`, so the real
  number is a question for whoever reads every body, not for a regular expression.

